      "why": "Under D-47 only the part of the insurance that the months before it is due cannot cover is held back. Her usual month leaves £2,500 - £1,000 - £800 = £700, and 4 whole months pass before 1 March 2027 (paydays on 1 November, 1 December, 1 January and 1 February), so they bring in 4 x £700 = £2,800, more than the £1,200 bill. Hold-back = the larger of £0 and £1,200 - £2,800, so £0 (the cap, the £700 built up over the 7 months since the last payment, is not reached). She has £1,500 free, so £1,500 - £1,450 = £50 is left this month. That is under the £180 floor (a tenth of £1,000 bills + £800 everyday spending), so the answer is \"Fits, but tight\" (D-40). The coming bill shows as a fact: \"Your £1,200 car insurance is due on 1 March 2027.\" (yearly_bill_ahead). On the cash path her lowest point is £50 on 31 October; by 28 February she has £2,850 before the bill is taken. She has no safety net, so the no-net line shows as a fact (net_none_start; tight comes first, D-35). The built-up share resets after each payment. Under the old FIN-11 rule (hold back the whole £700) this was \"Not yet\"; the owner replaced that rule (D-47). Someone whose usual month leaves too little to cover the bill still gets the full built-up share held back.",
